                "added": [
                    "#What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.<ref>https://www.swtc.edu/Ag_Power/electrical/lecture/series_circuits.htm</ref>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>  + .... R<sub>n</sub> <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example, consider finding the equivalent resistance in the image below<ref>https://www.kitronik.co.uk/blog/how-to-calculate-resistors-in-series-and-parallel/</ref><br><br>",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected<ref>http://physics.bu.edu/py106/notes/Circuits.html</ref>.[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The equation for combining ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/R<sub>1</sub>)+(1/R<sub>2</sub>)+(1/R<sub>3</sub>)..+(1/R<sub>n</sub>)}<ref>http://physics.bu.edu/py106/notes/Circuits.html</ref>",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together.<ref>https://www.electronics-tutorials.ws/resistor/res_5.html</ref> Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "== References ==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "*''Handbook for Sound Engineers: The New Audio Cyclopedia'', Glen Ballou, Editor",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>  + .... R<sub>n</sub> <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example, consider finding the equivalent resistance in the image below<br><br>",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected.[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The equation for combining ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/R<sub>1</sub>)+(1/R<sub>2</sub>)+(1/R<sub>3</sub>)..+(1/R<sub>n</sub>)}",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together. Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "== References ==",
                    "*''Handbook for Sound Engineers: The New Audio Cyclopedia'', Glen Ballou, Editor",
                    "*[http://physics.bu.edu/~duffy/PY106/Resistance.html Boston University]"
                ]
            }
        },
