            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together. Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
