        {
            "id": "12005177",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "07:13, 5 December 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,235 bytes)",
            "change": -493,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.",
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>  + .... R<sub>n</sub> <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example, consider finding the equivalent resistance in the image below<br><br>",
                    "#*In this example,<br> R<sub>1</sub> = 100 Ω and R<sub>2</sub> = 300Ω are wired in series. R<sub>eq</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω<br><br>",
                    "#[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected.",
                    "#*Here is an example, given R<sub>1</sub> = 20 Ω, R<sub>2</sub> = 30 Ω, and R<sub>3</sub> = 30 Ω.<br><br>",
                    "#*The total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br><br>   = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br><br>   = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57 Ω.<br><br>",
                    "#[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together. Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.<br><br>",
                    "#* We see the resistors R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> are connected in series. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>s</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω.<br><br>",
                    "#* Next, we see the resistors R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>4</sub> are connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p1</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p1</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω<br><br>",
                    "#* Then we see the resistors R<sub>5</sub> and R<sub>6</sub> are also connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p2</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p2</sub> = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω<br><br>",
                    "#* So now we have a circuit with the resistors R<sub>s</sub>, R<sub>p1</sub>, R<sub>p2</sub> and R<sub>7</sub> connected in series. These can now simply be added to get the equivalent resistance R<sub>7</sub> of the network given to us originally.<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 400 Ω + 10 Ω + 8 Ω + 10 Ω = 428 Ω.<br><br>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.",
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>  + .... R<sub>n</sub> <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example, consider finding the equivalent resistance in the image below<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*In this example,<br> R<sub>1</sub> = 100 Ω and R<sub>2</sub> = 300Ω are wired in series. R<sub>eq</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected.",
                    "#*Here is an example, given R<sub>1</sub> = 20 Ω, R<sub>2</sub> = 30 Ω, and R<sub>3</sub> = 30 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*The total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br><br>   = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br><br>   = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together. Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* We see the resistors R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> are connected in series. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>s</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Next, we see the resistors R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>4</sub> are connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p1</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p1</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Then we see the resistors R<sub>5</sub> and R<sub>6</sub> are also connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p2</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p2</sub> = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* So now we have a circuit with the resistors R<sub>s</sub>, R<sub>p1</sub>, R<sub>p2</sub> and R<sub>7</sub> connected in series. These can now simply be added to get the equivalent resistance R<sub>7</sub> of the network given to us originally.<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 400 Ω + 10 Ω + 8 Ω + 10 Ω = 428 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet4.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
