        {
            "id": "11628358",
            "user": "WikiHowUser20221012045502",
            "timestamp": "18:43, 31 October 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,578 bytes)",
            "change": -60,
            "summary": "(Grammar)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Need to know how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network? If you don't want to fry your circuit board, you do! This article will show you how in just a few easy steps.  Before reading this, please understand that resistors do not actually have an \"inside\" and an \"outside\" to them.  The use of \"in\" and \"out\" is merely a figure of speech to help novices understand the wiring concepts.",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected.",
                    "#*The equation for combining ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/R<sub>1</sub>)+(1/R<sub>2</sub>)+(1/R<sub>3</sub>)..+(1/R<sub>n</sub>)}",
                    "#* We see the resistors R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> are connected in series. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>s</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Next, we see the resistors R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>4</sub> are connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p1</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p1</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Then we see the resistors R<sub>5</sub> and R<sub>6</sub> are also connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p2</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p2</sub> = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#How you wire multiple resistors together makes much difference on the overall performance of a resistive network.",
                    "* Remember, when resistors are in parallel, there are many different means to an end, so the total resistance will be smaller than each pathway. When resistors are in series, the current will have to travel through each resistor, so the individual resistors will add to give the total resistance for the series."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Need to know how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network? If you don't want to fry your circuit board, you do! This article will show you how in just a few easy steps.  Before reading this, please understand that resistors do not actually have an \"in side\" and an \"out side\" to them.  The use of \"in\" and \"out\" is merely a figure of speech to help novices understand the wiring concepts.",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected together, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected together.",
                    "#*The equation for combining a total of ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/R<sub>1</sub>)+(1/R<sub>2</sub>)+(1/R<sub>3</sub>)..+(1/R<sub>n</sub>)}",
                    "#* We see that the resistors R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> are connected in series. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>s</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Next, we see that the resistors R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>4</sub> are connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p1</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p1</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Then we see that the resistors R<sub>5</sub> and R<sub>6</sub> are also connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p2</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p2</sub> = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#How you wire multiple resistors together makes a great deal of difference on the overall performance of a resistive network.",
                    "* Remember, when resistors are in parallel, there are many different means to an end, so the total resistance will be smaller than each pathway. When resistors are in series, the current will have to travel through each resistor, so the individual resistors will add up to give the total resistance for the series."
                ]
            }
        },
