        {
            "id": "11495146",
            "user": "204.10.221.76",
            "timestamp": "16:02, 22 October 2013",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(empty)",
            "change": -6640,
            "summary": "(Removing all content from page)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "reverted_by": "EpcotMagic",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [],
                "removed": [
                    "Need to know how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network? If you don't want to fry your circuit board, you do! This article will show you how in just a few easy steps.  Before reading this, please understand that resistors do not actually have an \"in side\" and an \"out side\" to them.  The use of \"in\" and \"out\" is merely a figure of speech to help novices understand the wiring concepts.",
                    "[[Category:Mathematics]]",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Help Calculating Resistance ===",
                    "[[Doc:Series Resistance,Parallel Resistance]]",
                    "===Series Resistance===",
                    "#What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.",
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>  + .... R<sub>n</sub> <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example, consider finding the equivalent resistance in the image below<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*In this example,<br> R<sub>1</sub> = 100 Ω and R<sub>2</sub> = 300Ω are wired in series. R<sub>eq</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 1Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "===Parallel Resistance===",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected together, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected together.",
                    "#*The equation for combining a total of ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/R<sub>1</sub>)+(1/R<sub>2</sub>)+(1/R<sub>3</sub>)..+(1/R<sub>n</sub>)}",
                    "#*Here is an example, given R<sub>1</sub> = 20 Ω, R<sub>2</sub> = 30 Ω, and R<sub>3</sub> = 30 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*The total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br><br>   = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br><br>   = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 2Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "===Combined Series and Parallel Circuits===",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together. Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* We see that the resistors R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> are connected in series. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>s</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Next, we see that the resistors R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>4</sub> are connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p1</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p1</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Then we see that the resistors R<sub>5</sub> and R<sub>6</sub> are also connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p2</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p2</sub> = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* So now we have a circuit with the resistors R<sub>s</sub>, R<sub>p1</sub>, R<sub>p2</sub> and R<sub>7</sub> connected in series. These can now simply be added to get the equivalent resistance R<sub>eq</sub> of the network given to us originally.<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 400 Ω + 10 Ω + 8 Ω + 10 Ω = 428 Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Calculate Series and Parallel Resistance Step 3Bullet4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "==Some Facts==",
                    "#Understand resistance. Every material that conducts electrical current has resistivity, which is the resistance of a material to electrical current.",
                    "#Resistance is measured in '''ohms'''. The symbol used for ohms is Ω.",
                    "#Different materials have different resistance properties.",
                    "#*Copper, for example, has a resistivity of 0.0000017(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#*Ceramics have a resistivity around 10<sup>14</sup>(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#The higher the number, the greater the resistance to electrical current. You can see that copper, which is commonly used in electrical wiring, has a very low resistivity. Ceramic, on the other hand, is so resistive that it makes an excellent insulator.",
                    "#How you wire multiple resistors together makes a great deal of difference on the overall performance of a resistive network.",
                    "#V=IR. This is Ohm's law, defined by Georg Ohm in the early 1800s. If you know any two of these variables, you can easily calculate the third.",
                    "#*V=IR: Voltage (V) is the product of current (I) * resistance (R).",
                    "#*I=V/R: Current is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ resistance (R).",
                    "#*R=V/I: Resistance is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ current(I).",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "* The equivalent resistance (Req) is always smaller than the smallest contributor for a parallel circuit; it is always greater than the greatest contributor for a series circuit.",
                    "* Remember, when resistors are in parallel, there are many different means to an end, so the total resistance will be smaller than each pathway. When resistors are in series, the current will have to travel through each resistor, so the individual resistors will add up to give the total resistance for the series.",
                    "",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "*''Handbook for Sound Engineers: The New Audio Cyclopedia'', Glen Ballou, Editor",
                    "*[http://physics.bu.edu/~duffy/PY106/Resistance.html Boston University]",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "*  [[Add Fractions With Unlike Denominators|How to Add Fractions With Unlike Denominators]]",
                    "*  [[Find the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Integers|How to Find the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Integers]]",
                    "*  [[Build a Parallel Circuit|How to Build a Parallel Circuit]]",
                    "*  [[Read Resistors|How to Read Resistors]]",
                    "*  [[Analyze Resistive Circuits Using Ohm's Law|How to Analyze Resistive Circuits Using Ohm's Law]]",
                    "*  [[Measure Resistance|How to Measure Resistance]]",
                    "[[es:calcular resistencias en serie y en paralelo]]",
                    "[[de:Wie man in Reihe und Parallel geschalteten Widerstand berechnet]]",
                    "[[pt:Calcular Resistências em Série e em Paralelo]]",
                    "[[fr:calculer la valeur de résistances en série ou en dérivation]]",
                    "[[it:Calcolare Resistenze in Serie e in Parallelo]]",
                    "[[nl:De weerstand van een schakeling berekenen]]"
                ]
            }
        },
