            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>  + .... R<sub>n</sub> <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example, consider finding the equivalent resistance in the image below",
                    "#*In this example,<br> R<sub>1</sub> = 100 Ω and R<sub>2</sub> = 300Ω are wired in series. R<sub>eq</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω",
                    "#*The equation for combining a total of ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/R<sub>1</sub>)+(1/R<sub>2</sub>)+(1/R<sub>3</sub>)..+(1/R<sub>n</sub>)}",
                    "#*Here is an example, given R<sub>1</sub> = 20 Ω, R<sub>2</sub> = 30 Ω, and R<sub>3</sub> = 30 Ω.",
                    "#*The total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br><br>   = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br><br>   = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57 Ω.",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together. Consider finding the equivalent resistance of the network shown below.",
                    "#* We see that the resistors R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> are connected in series. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>s</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>s</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> = 100 Ω + 300 Ω = 400 Ω.",
                    "#* Next, we see that the resistors R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>4</sub> are connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p1</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p1</sub> = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω",
                    "#* Then we see that the resistors R<sub>5</sub> and R<sub>6</sub> are also connected in parallel. So their equivalent resistance (let us denote it by R<sub>p2</sub>) is:<br><br> R<sub>p2</sub> = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω",
                    "#* So now we have a circuit with the resistors R<sub>s</sub>, R<sub>p1</sub>, R<sub>p2</sub> and R<sub>7</sub> connected in series. These can now simply be added to get the equivalent resistance R<sub>eq</sub> of the network given to us originally.<br><br>R<sub>eq</sub> = 400 Ω + 10 Ω + 8 Ω + 10 Ω = 428 Ω.",
                    "==Some Facts==",
                    "#Resistance is measured in '''ohms'''. The symbol used for ohms is Ω."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br>     Req = R1+R2+....Rn <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example:{{largeimage|Series_645.JPG}}",
                    "#*In this example, R1 (100Ω) and R2 (300Ω) are wired in series. Req = 100Ω+300Ω = 400Ω",
                    "#*The equation for combining a total of ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1/{(1/R1)+(1/R2)+(1/R3)..+(1/Rn)}",
                    "#*Here is an example, given R1 = 20Ω, R2 = 30Ω, and R3 = 30Ω.{{largeimage|Parallel_545.JPG}}",
                    "#*The total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1/{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br><br>Req = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br><br> Req = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57Ω.",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together into what's called \"parallel equivalent resistances\".",
                    "#*In the example below, we will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  Use two parallel bars \"||\" to mean \"in parallel with\".",
                    "#Calculate the parallel values. Define and calculate the 2 parallel equivalent resistors in the circuit, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\" as follows:",
                    "#*Rp1 = R3||R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω",
                    "#*Rp2 = R5||R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω{{largeimage|Combo1_90.JPG}}",
                    "#Add the series and parallel values. Combine the series circuit by adding the series resistances and the values calculated for the parallel circuits.{{largeimage|Combo2_958.JPG}}",
                    "#*Req = R1 + R2 + Rp1 + Rp2 + R7",
                    "#*Req = 100+300+10+8+100 = 518 Ω",
                    "===The Facts===",
                    "#Resistance is measured in '''ohms'''."
                ]
            }
        },
