            "id": "8133920",
            "user": "Rdiercks",
            "timestamp": "20:58, 23 July 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(4,893 bytes)",
            "change": 220,
            "summary": "(Correcting the use of \"in\" and \"out\" as it relates to resistors.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Need to know how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network? If you don't want to fry your circuit board, you do! This article will show you how in just a few easy steps.  Before reading this, please understand that resistors do not actually have an \"in side\" and an \"out side\" to them.  The use of \"in\" and \"out\" is merely a figure of speech to help novices understand the wiring concepts."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Need to know how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network? If you don't want to fry your circuit board, you do! This article will show you how in just a few easy steps."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "8068262",
            "user": "K53_PDX",
            "timestamp": "01:02, 11 July 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(4,673 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "(rearranged sections)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "",
                    "===The Facts===",
                    "#Understand resistance. Every material that conducts electrical current has resistivity, which is the resistance of a material to electrical current.",
                    "#Resistance is measured in '''ohms'''.",
                    "#Different materials have different resistance properties.",
                    "#*Copper, for example, has a resistivity of 0.0000017(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#*Ceramics have a resistivity around 10<sup>14</sup>(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#The higher the number, the greater the resistance to electrical current. You can see that copper, which is commonly used in electrical wiring, has a very low resistivity. Ceramic, on the other hand, is so resistive that it makes an excellent insulator.",
                    "#How you wire multiple resistors together makes a great deal of difference on the overall performance of a resistive network.",
                    "#V=IR. This is Ohm's law, defined by Georg Ohm in the early 1800s. If you know any two of these variables, you can easily calculate the third.",
                    "#*V=IR: Voltage (V) is the product of current (I) * resistance (R).",
                    "#*I=V/R: Current is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ resistance (R).",
                    "#*R=V/I: Resistance is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ current(I).",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "===The Facts===",
                    "",
                    "#Understand resistance. Every material that conducts electrical current has resistivity, which is the resistance of a material to electrical current.",
                    "#Resistance is measured in '''ohms'''.",
                    "#Different materials have different resistance properties.",
                    "#*Copper, for example, has a resistivity of 0.0000017(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#*Ceramics have a resistivity around 10<sup>14</sup>(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#The higher the number, the greater the resistance to electrical current. You can see that copper, which is commonly used in electrical wiring, has a very low resistivity. Ceramic, on the other hand, is so resistive that it makes an excellent insulator.",
                    "#How you wire multiple resistors together makes a great deal of difference on the overall performance of a resistive network.",
                    "#V=IR. This is Ohm's law, defined by Georg Ohm in the early 1800s. If you know any two of these variables, you can easily calculate the third.",
                    "#*V=IR: Voltage (V) is the product of current (I) * resistance (R).",
                    "#*I=V/R: Current is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ resistance (R).",
                    "#*R=V/I: Resistance is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ current(I)."
                ]
            }
        },
