        {
            "id": "8005921",
            "user": "K53_PDX",
            "timestamp": "16:24, 29 June 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(4,365 bytes)",
            "change": 1928,
            "summary": "(cleaned up the formatting, added some clarifying language. added an introduction to resistance and ohm's law; sourced audio bible)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "reverted_by": "BR",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Need to know how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network? If you don't want to fry your circuit board, you do! This article will show you how in just a few easy steps.",
                    "",
                    "===The Facts===",
                    "",
                    "#Understand resistance. Every material that conducts electrical current has resistivity, which is the resistance of a material to electrical current.",
                    "#Resistance is measured in '''ohms'''.",
                    "#Different materials have different resistance properties.",
                    "#*Copper, for example, has a resistivity of 0.0000017(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#*Ceramics have a resistivity around 10<sup>14</sup>(Ω/cm<sup>3</sup>)",
                    "#The higher the number, the greater the resistance to electrical current. You can see that copper, which is commonly used in electrical wiring, has a very low resistivity. Ceramic, on the other hand, is so resistive that it makes an excellent insulator.",
                    "#How you wire multiple resistors together makes a great deal of difference on the overall performance of a resistive network.",
                    "#V=IR. This is Ohm's law, defined by Georg Ohm in the early 1800s. If you know any two of these variables, you can easily calculate the third.",
                    "#*V=IR: Voltage (V) is the product of current (I) * resistance (R).",
                    "#*I=V/R: Current is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ resistance (R).",
                    "#*R=V/I: Resistance is the quotient of voltage (V) ÷ current(I).",
                    "",
                    "#What it is. Series resistance is simply connecting the \"out\" side of one resistor to the \"in\" side of another in a circuit. Each additional resistor placed in a circuit adds to the total resistance of that circuit.",
                    "#*The formula for calculating a total of ''n'' number of resistors wired in series is: <br>     Req = R1+R2+....Rn <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added. For example:<br><br>[[Image:Series_645.JPG|550px|center]]",
                    "#*In this example, R1 (100Ω) and R2 (300Ω) are wired in series. Req = 100Ω+300Ω = 400Ω",
                    "#What it is. Parallel resistance is when the \"in\" side of 2 or more resistors are connected together, and the \"out\" side of those resistors are connected together.",
                    "#*The equation for combing a total of ''n'' resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1/{(1/R1)+(1/R2)+(1/R3)..+(1/Rn)}",
                    "#*Here is an example, given R1 = 20Ω, R2 = 30Ω, and R3 = 30Ω.<br><br>[[Image:Parallel_545.JPG|550px|center]]",
                    "#*The total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br><br>Req = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br><br> Req = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57Ω.",
                    "#What it is. A combined network is any combination of series and parallel circuits wired together into what's called \"parallel equivalent resistances\".",
                    "#*In the example below, we will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  Use two parallel bars \"||\" to mean \"in parallel with\".",
                    "#Calculate the parallel values. Define and calculate the 2 parallel equivalent resistors in the circuit, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\" as follows:",
                    "#*Rp1 = R3||R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω",
                    "#*Rp2 = R5||R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω<br><br>[[Image:Combo1_90.JPG|550px|center]]",
                    "#Add the series and parallel values. Combine the series circuit by adding the series resistances and the values calculated for the parallel circuits.<br><br>[[Image:Combo2_958.JPG|550px|center]]",
                    "#*Req = R1 + R2 + Rp1 + Rp2 + R7",
                    "#*Req = 100+300+10+8+100 = 518 Ω",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "*''Handbook for Sound Engineers: The New Audio Cyclopedia'', Glen Ballou, Editor",
                    "*[http://physics.bu.edu/~duffy/PY106/Resistance.html Boston University]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "The following will demonstrate how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network. For the examples, please refer to the given figures.",
                    "*  The formula for combining a total of \"n\" series resistors into a single equivalent resistor \"Req\" is: <br>Req = R1+R2+....Rn <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added.",
                    "<br>",
                    "*   For the example shown below: Req = 100Ω+300Ω = 400Ω [[Image:Series_645.JPG|483px|center]]",
                    "*  The equation for combing a total of \"n\" resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1/{(1/R1)+(1/R2)+(1/R3)..+(1/Rn)}",
                    "<br>",
                    "*  For the example below there are 3 resistors (n = 3) with R1 = 20Ω, R2 = 30Ω, and R3 = 30Ω.  Using the above formula we find the total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br> = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br> = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57Ω. [[Image:Parallel_545.JPG|576px|center]]",
                    "# Combine any resistors that are in parallel into \"parallel equivalent resistances\".  We will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  We use two parallel bars \"//\" to mean \"in parallel with\".  In the example below we define 2 new parallel equivalent resistors, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\": <br> Rp1 = R3//R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω <br> Rp2 = R5//R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω [[Image:Combo1_90.JPG|669px|right]]",
                    "# Combine the resulting series circuit by adding the series resistances.  In this example: <br> Req = R1 + R2 + Rp1 + Rp2 + R7 <br> = 100+300+10+8+100 = 518 Ω [[Image:Combo2_958.JPG|669px|right]]",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
