            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "# Combine any resistors that are in parallel into \"parallel equivalent resistances\".  We will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  We use two parallel bars \"//\" to mean \"in parallel with\".  In the example below we define 2 new parallel equivalent resistors, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\": <br> Rp1 = R3//R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω <br> Rp2 = R5//R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω [[Image:Combo1_90.JPG|669px|right]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Combine any resistors that are in parallel innnnto \"parallel equivalent resistances\".  We will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  We use two parallel bars \"//\" to mean \"in parallel with\".  In the example below we define 2 new parallel equivalent resistors, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\": <br> Rp1 = R3//R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω <br> Rp2 = R5//R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω [[Image:Combo1_90.JPG|669px|right]]"
                ]
            }
