                "added": [
                    "# Combine any resistors that are in parallel into \"parallel equivalent resistances\".  We will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  We use two parallel bars \"//\" to mean \"in parallel with\".  In the example below we define 2 new parallel equivalent resistors, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\": <br> Rp1 = R3//R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω <br> Rp2 = R5//R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/40) = 40/5 = 8 Ω [[Image:Combo1_90.JPG|669px|right]]",
                    "# Combine the resulting series circuit by adding the series resistances.  In this example: <br> Req = R1 + R2 + Rp1 + Rp2 + R7 <br> = 100+300+10+8+100 = 518 Ω [[Image:Combo2_958.JPG|669px|right]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Combine any resistors that are in parallel into \"parallel equivalent resistances\".  We will denote parallel equivalent resistances with a \"p\" subscript.  We use two parallel bars \"//\" to mean \"in parallel with\".  In the example below we define 2 new parallel equivalent resistors, \"Rp1\" and \"Rp2\": <br> Rp1 = R3//R4 = 1/{(1/20)+(1/20)} = 1/(2/20)= 20/2 = 10 Ω <br> Rp2 = R5//R6 = 1/{(1/40)+(1/10)} = 1/(5/10) = 10/5 = 2 Ω [[Image:Combo1_90.JPG|669px|right]]",
                    "# Combine the resulting series circuit by adding the series resistances.  In this example: <br> Req = R1 + R2 + Rp1 + Rp2 + R7 <br> = 100+300+10+2+100 = 512 Ω [[Image:Combo2_958.JPG|669px|right]]"
