        {
            "id": "6717572",
            "user": "Carterchas",
            "timestamp": "10:33, 7 October 2011",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(2,375 bytes)",
            "change": -186,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by 46.16.3.241 (Talk); changed back to last version by TheMartian)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "reverted_by": "BR",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "The following will demonstrate how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network. For the examples, please refer to the given figures.",
                    "",
                    "===Series Resistance===",
                    "*  The formula for combining a total of \"n\" series resistors into a single equivalent resistor \"Req\" is: <br>Req = R1+R2+....Rn <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added.",
                    "<br>",
                    "*   For the example shown below: Req = 100Ω+300Ω = 400Ω [[Image:Series_645.JPG|483px|center]]",
                    "",
                    "===Parallel Resistance===",
                    "*  The equation for combing a total of \"n\" resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1/{(1/R1)+(1/R2)+(1/R3)..+(1/Rn)}",
                    "<br>",
                    "*  For the example below there are 3 resistors (n = 3) with R1 = 20Ω, R2 = 30Ω, and R3 = 30Ω.  Using the above formula we find the total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br> = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br> = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57Ω. [[Image:Parallel_545.JPG|576px|center]]",
                    "",
                    "===Combined Series and Parallel Circuits===",
                    "* The equivalent resistance (Req) is always smaller than the smallest contributor for a parallel circuit; it is always greater than the greatest contributor for a series circuit.",
                    "* Remember, when resistors are in parallel, there are many different means to an end, so the total resistance will be smaller than each pathway. When resistors are in series, the current will have to travel through each resistor, so the individual resistors will add up to give the total resistance for the series.",
                    "* [[Add Fractions With Unlike Denominators]]",
                    "* [[Find the Greatest Common Divisor (Gcd) of Two Integers]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "The following will demonstrate how to calculate series resistance, parallel resistance, and a combined series and parallel network. For the examples, please refer to the given jghfinkol,bigojghjjghkjg,m          my name is bob the bilder figures.",
                    "#gfhtsrgdxf uuuuuuuuuuuuueathryaturyhtduryitdyitdyitsduryititd",
                    "=== Series Resistance ===",
                    "#*  The formula for combining a total of \"n\" series resistors into a single equivalent resistor \"Req\" is: <br>Req = R1+R2+....Rn <br> That is, all the series resistor values are simply added.",
                    "#<br>",
                    "#* jjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjdj",
                    "=== Parallel Resistance ===",
                    "#*  The equation for combing a total of \"n\" resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1/{(1/R1)+(1/R2)+(1/R3)..+(1/Rn)}",
                    "#<br>",
                    "#*  For the example below there are 3 resistors (n = 3) with R1 = 20Ω, R2 = 30Ω, and R3 = 30Ω.  Using the above formula we find the total equivalent resistance for all 3 resistors in parallel is:<br>Req = 1{(1/20)+(1/30)+(1/30)} <br> = 1/{(3/60)+(2/60)+(2/60)} <br> = 1/(7/60)=60/7 Ω = approximately 8.57Ω. [[Image:Parallel_545.JPG|576px|center]]",
                    "=== Combined Series and Parallel Circuits ===",
                    "*The equivalent resistance (Req) is always smaller than the smallest contributor for a parallel circuit; it is always greater than the greatest contributor for a series circuit.",
                    "*Remember, when resistors are in parallel, there are many different means to an end, so the total resistance will be smaller than each pathway. When resistors are in series, the current will have to travel through each resistor, so the individual resistors will add up to give the total resinmnmbnmbnmnmnbmnstance for the series.",
                    "*[[Add Fractions With Unlike Denominators|How to Add Fractions With Unlike Denominators]]",
                    "*[[Find the Greatest Common Divisor (Gcd) of Two Integers|How to Find the Greatest Common Divisor (Gcd) of Two Integers]]"
                ]
            }
        },
